heavy-right-handed-neutrino-mass-scale-9.interpretation:
The relation $m_N \approx M$ is a key assumption in the Type-I seesaw mechanism, which aims to explain the smallness of observed neutrino masses ($m_{
u}$). In this framework, the light mass $m_{
u}$ is inversely proportional to the heavy mass $m_N$ via the relation $m_{
u} \approx m_D^2 / m_N$, where $m_D$ is a Dirac mass term typically associated with the electroweak scale. By positing that $m_N$ is tied to a very large fundamental scale $M$ (e.g., the GUT scale, $M_{GUT} \sim 10^{16}$ GeV), the seesaw mechanism naturally generates a very small mass for $m_{
u}$, consistent with experimental observations. This approximation therefore forges a direct link between low-energy neutrino phenomena and new physics at ultra-high energy scales.
